# 23. Virtual environments and pip

One of the most important things I learned is that pip is commonly used together with **virtual environments**.

Imagine you have two projects:

```text
Project A
    requests version X

Project B
    requests version Y
```

If both projects use the same global Python environment, package versions can conflict.

A virtual environment gives each project its own isolated environment.

Think of it as:

```text
Computer
│
├── Project A
│   └── Virtual Environment
│       └── Packages
│
└── Project B
    └── Virtual Environment
        └── Packages
```

---

# 24. Create a virtual environment

First create a project directory:

```bash
mkdir myproject
```

Move into it:

```bash
cd myproject
```

Create a virtual environment:

```bash
python -m venv venv
```

Here:

```text
python
   ↓
-m venv
   ↓
create virtual environment
   ↓
venv
```

`venv` is the name of the environment.

You can choose another name.

---

# 25. Activate the virtual environment on Linux

Run:

```bash
source venv/bin/activate
```

After activation, your terminal may look similar to:

```text
(venv) user@computer:~/myproject$
```

The `(venv)` tells you that the virtual environment is active.

Now when you install packages:

```bash
python -m pip install requests
```

the package is installed into that environment.

---

# 26. Activate on Windows

On Windows Command Prompt:

```cmd
venv\Scripts\activate
```

On PowerShell:

```powershell
venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

After activation, you should see something similar to:

```text
(venv)
```

in your terminal prompt.

---

# 27. Deactivate the virtual environment

When you're finished:

```bash
deactivate
```

This returns you to the normal environment.

---

# 28. Why virtual environments matter

Without virtual environments:

```text
Project A
   ↓
Global packages

Project B
   ↓
Global packages
```

Packages can interfere with each other.

With virtual environments:

```text
Project A
   ↓
Environment A
   ↓
Packages A

Project B
   ↓
Environment B
   ↓
Packages B
```

Each project can have its own dependencies.

